Chapter: CharAtBounds
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `char-at` and `char-code-at` trap on an index outside the text. This file
 is the half a battery can fail on: every legal index must still answer, for
 both builtins, because the lexer reads one of them once per source byte and
 a guard that refuses a legal read would stop the compiler rather than a
 program.

 The bound is tighter than `substring`'s by one. A zero-length slice at
 start == length is legal and answers empty; there is no byte AT index
 length, so `char-at s (text-length s)` traps. That one-off is the easiest
 thing to get wrong in either direction, so the last legal index and the
 first illegal one are both named here -- the legal one below, the illegal
 one in `codex/test/char-at-past-end.codex`.

 The two trap demonstrations are separate files because the two guards are
 separate: a negative index and an index past the end fail for different
 reasons and neither can stand in for the other. Both carry a `.fatal`,
 which means they are compile checks plus a manual read; `-Fatal` is not
 part of any routine battery.

Section: Subject

  s : Text = "abcde"

Section: Report

  walk : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  walk (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else walk (i + 1) n (acc & " " & show i & "=" & show (char-code-at s i))

  walk-chars : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  walk-chars (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else walk-chars (i + 1) n (acc & char-to-text (char-at s i))

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("codes:" & walk 0 (text-length s) "")
    print-line-uni ("chars=[" & walk-chars 0 (text-length s) "" & "]")
    print-line-uni ("last-legal=" & show (char-code-at s (text-length s - 1)))
    print-line-uni ("first=" & show (char-code-at s 0))
  end

